(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-inflammation-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Inflammation — Quiz 2","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which three cell types are granulocytes?","choices":["Lymphocytes, monocytes and macrophages","Neutrophils, basophils and eosinophils","Neutrophils, monocytes and B lymphocytes","Basophils, eosinophils and natural killer cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the three granulocytes; polymorphonuclear leukocyte refers to all granulocytes. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why is the name agranulocyte somewhat misleading?","choices":["Because these cells acquire granules only after activation","Because their granules are visible only in chronic inflammation","Because they do contain azurophilic granules — lysosomes","Because they are actually a subtype of polymorphonuclear leukocyte"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The name implies an absence of granules, but non-specific azurophilic granules (lysosomes) are present. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which description matches the appearance of a neutrophil?","choices":["Bi-lobed nucleus with pink-orange granules","Kidney-shaped nucleus with abundant cytoplasm that is usually agranulated when it is viewed on light microscopy","Multilobed nucleus, with fine pale lilac granules making the cytoplasm look transparent","Bi- or tri-lobed nucleus with a number of coarse granules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil's multilobed nucleus can look like several nuclei, and fine pale lilac granules make its cytoplasm look transparent. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is the first responder to microbial infection?","choices":["The macrophage","The eosinophil","The B lymphocyte","The neutrophil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil is the first responder to microbial infection, defending against bacterial and fungal infection. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Trauma versus a bee sting illustrates which distinction within acute inflammation?","choices":["Purely physical damage versus damage that activates an immune response","Inflammation that resolves completely versus inflammation that becomes chronic","Serous versus fibrinous exudate","Endogenous versus exogenous toxic agents"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In acute inflammation, damage may be purely physical, as in trauma, or may involve activation of an immune response, as in a bee sting. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which pair of conditions are examples of autoimmunity causing chronic inflammation?","choices":["Atherosclerosis and silicosis","Asthma and hay fever","Trauma and bee sting","Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus are autoimmune diseases, and autoimmunity is one of the three causes of chronic inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is a biomaterial?","choices":["Any tissue harvested from the patient's own body","Any protein produced during the inflammatory response","Anything not human that is put into a human","Any material that has been sterilized before implantation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A biomaterial is anything not human put into a human, and it becomes coated with immunoglobulin G and C3b once implanted. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which property distinguishes the monocyte from the neutrophil in terms of sustained phagocytic capacity?","choices":["The monocyte has a multilobed nucleus permitting repeated division","The monocyte can replace its lysosomal contents","The monocyte does not require opsonization of its targets","The monocyte carries T cell receptors for direct antigen binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil cannot renew its lysosomes and dies after a few pathogens; the monocyte can replace its lysosomal contents and so keeps working. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"In the sequence of events of acute inflammation, which vascular event comes FIRST?","choices":["Vasoconstriction","Vasodilation","Increased vascular permeability","Hemoconcentration and stasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The sequence opens with vasoconstriction, followed by vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, then hemoconcentration and stasis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which pairing of endogenous and exogenous toxic agents in chronic inflammation is correct?","choices":["Endogenous — silica; exogenous — atherosclerosis","Endogenous — rheumatoid arthritis; exogenous — lupus","Endogenous — atherosclerosis; exogenous — particulates such as silica","Endogenous — bacterial infection; exogenous — viral infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atherosclerosis is the endogenous example and particulates such as silica the exogenous one. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which pairing of infection type and predominant reaction describes the eosinophil?","choices":["Bacterial infection; predominating in suppurative inflammation","Parasitic infection; predominating in allergic reactions such as asthma","Viral infection; predominating in cell-mediated immunity","Fungal infection; predominating in chronic granulomatous inflammatory processes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Eosinophils primarily deal with parasitic infections and are the predominant inflammatory cells in allergic reactions such as asthma, hay fever and hives. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How do phagocytic cells recognize immunoglobulin G bound to foreign material?","choices":["Through the complement receptors that bind to the C3b fragment","Through T cell receptors on the phagocyte surface","Through histamine receptors on the endothelium","Through Fc receptors binding the Fc portion of the antibody"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages and neutrophils carry Fc receptors that bind the Fc portion of the antibody. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which statement describes the timing of acute inflammation?","choices":["Over days to months, beginning once macrophages arrive","Over weeks, beginning after antibody production starts","Over minutes to hours only, beginning after a full 24 hours have elapsed since the injury","Over seconds, minutes, hours or days, beginning within seconds of tissue injury"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute inflammation begins immediately — within seconds — following injury of tissues. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which vascular change is directly responsible for allowing fluid and blood proteins to enter the interstitium?","choices":["Vasodilation of the supplying arterioles","Vasoconstriction of the draining venules","Endothelial contraction increasing capillary permeability","Hemoconcentration occurring within the capillary bed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Endothelial contraction is what allows the increased permeability through which exudation occurs. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which statement about histamine in the inflammatory response is correct?","choices":["The final mediator released during the whole response, causing fibrosis and the formation of dense scar tissue at the site","The first mediator of the response, dilating arterioles and increasing capillary and venule permeability","A chemotactic factor drawing neutrophils to the site","An opsonin that coats foreign material for recognition"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Histamine is the first mediator of the initial response, dilating arterioles and increasing capillary and venule permeability. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which pairing of share of circulating serum proteins and action is correct for complement components?","choices":["10%; chemotactic to neutrophils and monocytes, with the cascade able to damage bacteria","50%; primarily responsible for oncotic pressure","1%; acting solely as opsonins","25%; responsible for the formation of a clot at the site of the injury or lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Complement components make up 10% of circulating serum proteins, are chemotactic to neutrophils and monocytes, and can damage bacteria. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Through which two routes can the inflammatory process heal an injury or implant site?","choices":["Vasoconstriction of the supplying arterioles, or lymphatic drainage of the exudate","New tissue from native parenchymal cells, or fibroblastic scar tissue","Phagocytosis by neutrophils alone, or opsonization by immunoglobulin G","Hemoconcentration followed by stasis, or leukocyte transmigration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The site heals either by new tissue generated by native parenchymal cells or by formation of fibroblastic scar tissue. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What do B lymphocytes do?","choices":["Cell-mediated killing of virus-infected cells through T cell receptors","Returning immune function to its normal operation after infection, and preventing autoimmunity","Releasing histamine to increase vascular permeability","Making antibodies, presenting antigen, and becoming memory B cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. B lymphocytes make antibodies, act as antigen-presenting cells and become memory B cells; they drive the humoral immune response. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which mediator is distinguished from histamine and serotonin by also producing pain?","choices":["Complement component C3b","Immunoglobulin G","Fibrinogen","Bradykinin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three raise permeability, but only bradykinin also causes pain, along with a possible increase in leukocyte chemotaxis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Recognition and attachment during phagocytosis are enhanced by the presence of what?","choices":["Serum factors known as opsonins","Histamine released from basophils","Fibrinogen deposited by the coagulation system","Bradykinin generated from plasma proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Opsonins such as immunoglobulin G and complement fragment C3b enhance recognition and attachment during phagocytosis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which combination of effects does bradykinin produce?","choices":["Vasoconstriction, reduced capillary permeability, and analgesia at the site","Antibody production and antigen presentation","Coagulation and trapping of exudate","Increased capillary permeability, pain, and possibly leukocyte chemotaxis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bradykinin is the plasma-protein mediator that adds pain to the permeability effect. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which description matches the serous pattern of inflammation?","choices":["Largely plasma with low protein content, occurring early or with mild inflammation","Large amounts of fibrinogen forming a thick meshwork","Remnants of white blood cells, protein and tissue debris","Mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Serous inflammation is largely plasma, low in protein, and occurs early or when inflammation is mild. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Beyond phagocytosis, what is the monocyte's role in the immune response?","choices":["Secreting histamine to open the capillary bed","Producing the fibrous network that traps exudate","Presenting pathogen pieces to T cells so it can be recognized again and killed","Restoring the immune system to its normal function once the infection has resolved"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After phagocytosis, monocytes present pieces of pathogens to T cells so that the pathogens may be recognized again and killed. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which cells are the agranulocytes (mononuclear leucocytes)?","choices":["Neutrophils, eosinophils and basophils","Erythrocytes, platelets and circulating plasma cells","Basophils, mast cells and macrophages","Lymphocytes (B cells and T cells), monocytes and macrophages"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These three are grouped as the agranulocytes or mononuclear leucocytes. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which statement about neutrophils is correct?","choices":["They are the predominant inflammatory cell in allergic reactions and in parasitic infection","They respond first to microbial infection, and their mass death forms pus","They mature in the thymus and carry T cell receptors","They present antigen to T cells after phagocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are first responders to microbial infection, and their activity and death in large numbers forms pus. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Digestion of foreign material by activated neutrophils and macrophages involves which four steps?","choices":["Vasodilation, exudation, stasis, and repair","Adhesion, transmigration, aggregation, and chemotaxis","Recognition, attachment, engulfment, and degradation","Opsonization, complement fixation, lysis, and clearance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those four, and recognition and attachment are enhanced when opsonins are present. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In chronic inflammation, what roles do lymphocytes and plasma cells versus macrophages play?","choices":["Lymphocytes phagocytose the cellular debris at the site, while macrophages produce and secrete the antibody","Both populations act solely to proliferate blood vessels","Lymphocytes and plasma cells make antibody; macrophages process and deliver antigen","Lymphocytes release histamine; macrophages release serotonin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibody production belongs to lymphocytes and plasma cells, antigen processing and delivery to macrophages. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which pairing of maturation site and surface structure is correct for T lymphocytes?","choices":["The thymus; T cell receptors","The bone marrow; Fc receptors","The spleen; complement receptors","The lymph nodes; major histocompatibility complexes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. T lymphocytes mature in the thymus, which gives them their name, and carry T cell receptors on their surface. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Ulcerative inflammation is characterized by which change?","choices":["Necrosis and sloughing of surface epithelium, exposing underlying connective tissue","Formation of a membrane-like covering over intact and otherwise viable mucosa","Damage to blood vessels occurring with other exudates","Coagulative necrosis of an entire limb segment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That exposure of the underlying connective tissue is what defines the ulcerative pattern. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are the three causes of chronic inflammation?","choices":["Trauma, thermal injury, and radiation exposure","Persistent infection, prolonged exposure to potentially toxic agents, and autoimmunity","Vasodilation, increased permeability, and exudation","Opsonization of foreign material, phagocytosis by neutrophils, and enzymatic degradation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Persistent infection, prolonged exposure to toxic agents and autoimmunity cause chronic inflammation; lupus is an autoimmune example. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"}]};
